## What the material implies for the analysis

- Estimator: π̂ = 4·k/n with k ~ Binomial(n, π/4). Its standard deviation is σ(n) = sqrt(π(4−π)/n) ≈ 1.642/sqrt(n); E|π̂−π| ≈ sqrt(2/π)·σ ≈ 1.310/sqrt(n). So the natural "1/sqrt(n) line" has the theoretical constant, not an arbitrary one; a line through the first point is also conventional.
- Four single-seed errors scatter strongly around this line (|error|/σ is half-normal: its 5–95 % range is ~0.06–1.96). A slope fit on four points therefore has a wide uncertainty; "follows the rate" is judged by (a) each point's standardised error z = |err|/σ(n) being consistent with a half-normal, (b) the log-log slope with its uncertainty compared with −1/2, and (c) a robustness check with many independent replicates per n, which measures the RMS error rate itself (clearly labelled as off-cluster supplementary computation).
- The cluster values are reproducible exactly from the seeds (Python's Mersenne Twister); recomputing them off-cluster checks the delivered numbers.

## Conventions

Log-log axes, error on y, n on x; reference line labelled 1/sqrt(n) with its constant stated; points marked, not joined only by lines. Numbers carry their provenance (cluster job id, output file).

